import{_ as a,o as t,c as n,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Playing Live","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"PLAYING_LIVE.md","filePath":"PLAYING_LIVE.md"}'),o={name:"PLAYING_LIVE.md"};function i(r,e,l,h,c,p){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="playing-live" tabindex="-1">Playing Live <a class="header-anchor" href="#playing-live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Playing Live&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A song can be played in real time while you work on it, and edited while it plays. This page covers playing from Arrange; <a href="./INSTRUMENTS">Instruments</a> covers setting up the sound, and <a href="./MIDI_SETUP">MIDI Setup</a> covers connecting a Mac to a synthesizer or Ableton Live and testing the connection.</p><h2 id="real-time-playback" tabindex="-1">Real-Time Playback <a class="header-anchor" href="#real-time-playback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Real-Time Playback&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Playing in real time is for composing: you hear the song through your synthesizers or FluidSynth as you work, and nothing about playing changes an export. To set it up, add an instrument on the Instruments page, <code>I</code> on the main screen, choosing one of this computer&#39;s MIDI output ports and a channel, or FluidSynth, and choose that instrument in each track&#39;s editor. Adding the first instrument turns the main screen&#39;s Real Time switch on, and <code>T</code> on the main screen turns it on and off.</p><p>While the switch is on, Arrange has its play controls: a <code>▶ song</code> button in the corner over the scene names, which the arrows reach; a <code>▶</code> beside each clip, in place of the left end of the clip&#39;s bar; and <code>Space</code> wherever the cursor is.</p><h2 id="playing-and-stopping" tabindex="-1">Playing and Stopping <a class="header-anchor" href="#playing-and-stopping" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Playing and Stopping&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong><code>Space</code> on the corner</strong> plays the song from the top, and <strong><code>Space</code> on a scene&#39;s name</strong> plays the song from that scene. Either way, every clip of the scene starts together. When the scene is done, each clip passes a start to the clip below it as its Advance says, as <a href="./CLIPS#advance">Clips</a> describes, and a scene with Launch together on starts as a whole when a start reaches any of its clips, stopping whatever the scene above still plays, loops included. With the defaults, the song plays from there to the end exactly as Live&#39;s Arrangement View plays the export. A scene whose every clip loops can&#39;t play, just as it can&#39;t export, since such a scene would wait forever.</li><li><strong><code>Space</code> on a clip</strong> plays that clip on its own, whatever its scene says, replacing whatever its track was playing, while the other tracks go on. <code>Space</code> on a playing clip stops its track.</li><li><strong>Every start by hand waits for the next bar</strong> while anything plays, as Live&#39;s default quantization does. The bars are counted in the song&#39;s time signature from the start of the earliest clip playing, the music goes on until then, and the status line says, for example, <code>scene 2 starts at the next bar</code>. A scene or clip shorter than a bar hands over at its end, and with nothing playing, a start happens at once.</li><li><strong><code>Space</code> again</strong> on the corner or on a playing scene stops everything, as <code>Ctrl+T</code> does on any page. The song keeps playing when you leave Arrange or open another page, so that you can edit it as it plays; only a listening page&#39;s preview stops when you leave that page. Stopping, like quitting, sends note offs for what was sounding, then all notes off and all sound off on each channel.</li><li><strong>A track with no instrument</strong> is silent in real time but still counts in the arrangement, so the song keeps its shape. If an instrument&#39;s device isn&#39;t connected, a dialog says which one, and nothing plays until another device is chosen on the Instruments page.</li></ul><h2 id="playback-lights" tabindex="-1">Playback Lights <a class="header-anchor" href="#playback-lights" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Playback Lights&quot;">​</a></h2><p>While something plays, Arrange shows what is playing:</p><ul><li>the corner reads <code>■ song</code>;</li><li>a playing clip&#39;s <code>▶</code> becomes a red <code>■</code>, and its repeat mark counts down the repeats left;</li><li>a green <code>●</code> follows a track&#39;s name while a note sounds on the track;</li><li>the playing scene&#39;s bar fills from the left as the song moves through the scene, and each playing clip&#39;s bar fills the same way through the clip&#39;s repeats, a looping clip&#39;s bar filling once per play of its scene and starting over;</li><li>the clock at the right of the crumbs reads, for example, <code>▶ 0:22 / 3:00</code>.</li></ul><p>These are llamatron&#39;s only colours, and the shapes change too, so the state shows without the colours.</p><h2 id="live-editing" tabindex="-1">Live Editing <a class="header-anchor" href="#live-editing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Live Editing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An edit made while something plays is heard on what plays, from the moment the song&#39;s Live response setting says: the next bar by default, each playing clip&#39;s next pass, or at once. Typed digits are heard once you stop typing, a song with no seed keeps its random choices for the whole play, and a scene whose every clip you make loop plays on until you launch another. While the song plays, a track or scene with clips can&#39;t be deleted, though anything can be inserted. None of this affects an export, which plays every scene once through.</p>',13)])])}const y=a(o,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,y as default};
